class FooterScriptManager {
  constructor(config = {}) {
    this.scripts = [];
    this.asyncMode = config.asyncMode || false;
    this.useDataIdAttribute = config.useDataIdAttribute !== false;
  }

  addScript(script) {
    if (!script.scriptId || !script.scriptContent) {
      console.warn('Invalid script object:', script);
      return;
    }

    const existingScript = this.scripts.find((s) => s.scriptId === script.scriptId);
    if (existingScript) {
      console.warn(`Duplicate script ID found: ${script.scriptId}`);
      return;
    }

    this.scripts.push(script);
  }

  render() {
    const footer = document.createElement('footer');

    if (this.useDataIdAttribute) {
      footer.setAttribute('data-script-id', 'footer-scripts');
    }

    this.scripts.forEach((script) => {
      const scriptElement = document.createElement('script');

      // Escape the script content to protect against XSS attacks
      scriptElement.textContent = DOMPurify.sanitize(script.scriptContent);
      scriptElement.setAttribute('id', script.scriptId);

      if (this.asyncMode) {
        scriptElement.async = true;
      }

      if (this.useDataIdAttribute) {
        scriptElement.dataset.scriptId = script.scriptId;
      }

      footer.appendChild(scriptElement);
    });

    document.body.appendChild(footer);
  }
}

// Example usage:
const manager = new FooterScriptManager({ asyncMode: true, useDataIdAttribute: false });
manager.addScript({ scriptId: 'google-analytics', scriptContent: 'GA_SCRIPT_CONTENT' });
manager.render();
```

For the third-party analytics service and CDN integration, you would need to extend the `FooterScriptManager` class by adding methods that handle loading and configuring these services. The actual implementation depends on the APIs provided by the specific services.

Regarding unit tests, I suggest using a testing framework like Jest or Mocha to create test cases for different scenarios such as empty arrays, invalid scripts, and very large arrays. Make sure to cover edge cases and error handling in your tests.

Lastly, remember to minify and optimize the code for production use. Good luck with your project!"]

