Regarding the Java function that takes an integer array as input and returns the number of disjoint pairs of elements in the array:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DisjointPairs {
    private int[] parents;

    public long countDisjointPairs(int[] arr) {
        UnionFind uf = new UnionFind(arr.length);
        long pairs = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; ++i) {
            int root = uf.find(i);
            long time = System.currentTimeMillis();
            if (uf.isConnected(root, i+1)) continue; // Skip disjoint pairs
            pairs += 1;
            System.out.println("Pair " + (i+1) + ", " + (i+2) + " checked in " + (System.currentTimeMillis() - time) + " ms.");
        }
        return pairs;
    }

    private class UnionFind {
        private int[] parent, rank;

        public UnionFind(int size) {
            this.parent = new int[size];
            this.rank = new int[size];
            for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
                parent[i] = i;
                rank[i] = 1;
            }
        }

        public int find(int x) {
            if (parent[x] != x) {
                parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
            }
            return parent[x];
        }

        public boolean isConnected(int p, int q) {
            return find(p) == find(q);
        }
    }
}
```

This solution makes trade-offs between efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability. The UnionFind data structure is efficient for checking disjointness, but it may not be the most memory-efficient solution for large arrays. However, since we are targeting a limited number of stocks (up to 5000 symbols), this approach should suffice. The Java code snippet provided above can be easily integrated into other parts of the microservice and used for various purposes, such as testing or performance analysis.']

